-- Sharing metadata: read by tooling and the mod manager's detail pane.
return {
  summary = "The battle command and move menus as four buttons in a 2x2 "
    .. "grid instead of a list, drawn in the Game Boy's own text boxes and "
    .. "inside the Game Boy's own screen. Dialogue still takes the bottom "
    .. "of the screen back for as long as it is talking.",
  author = "wild1walker",
  tags = { "ui", "battle", "menu", "buttons", "moves", "colour", "xp" },
  credits = {
    { who = "Gen1Recomp", for_ = "the three battle hooks this is built on -- "
      .. "battle.overlay, battle.bottom_ui_visible and "
      .. "battle.move_grid_navigation" },
    { who = "pret/pokered", for_ = "engine/battle/core.asm, whose "
      .. "DisplayBattleMenu and MoveSelectionMenu are the two screens this "
      .. "re-dresses, and whose 2x2 reading of the menu index is why the "
      .. "command menu needed no new input handling" },
    { who = "unxpected-uxp", for_ = "the XP bar, from their Quality of Life "
      .. "mod by way of Gen1WildQOL, which maintained it and wrote the faint "
      .. "guard it still carries" },
    { who = "Douglas Vautour", for_ = "Plain Pixel (CC-BY 4.0), the face "
      .. "Gen1Recomp bundles for its translation mode and this mod borrows "
      .. "for move names that will not fit the tile font" },
    { who = "Nintendo / Creatures / GAME FREAK", for_ = "Pokemon Red, Blue "
      .. "and Yellow.  Unofficial fan mod, no affiliation, no endorsement" },
  },
  compat = { engine = ">=0.0.0-0 <2.0.0", modApi = 2, games = { "gen1" } },
  differences = {
    changed = {
      "the two cursors come from the engine's own Theme.cursor and Theme.cursorHollow rather than being written out in the mod, so a skin that restyles the arrow restyles these with it",
      "the command menu is four bordered boxes in a 2x2 rather than four words in one box: four 10x3 boxes tiling the classic layout's twenty-by-six bottom strip exactly, which is what three tile rows per button and two rows of buttons comes to",
      "a button is Font.drawBox -- the same call, the same border glyphs and the same white interior every text box in the game is made of -- so a skin or a font mod that redraws the border redraws these buttons with it",
      "the move menu is the same grid rather than a vertical list of four, with the engine's own battle.move_grid_navigation answered so LEFT and RIGHT cross it",
      "each cell keeps its first interior tile for the cursor whether or not the cursor is there, so the three unselected labels do not slide sideways as it moves",
      "the classic move cell is seven glyphs against the vanilla list's fourteen, so long names are cut with the engine's own trailing-dot idiom and the trailing space taken off the cut: QUICK ATTACK reads QUICK.",
      "the widescreen layout gets the same grid as ONE box ruled into four cells with its own border glyphs, because five tile rows will not hold two three-row boxes without eating a row of battlefield; its cells are twelve glyphs, so names arrive whole there",
      "Mimic's copy menu is the same grid one tile row lower, matching the row the engine clips the player's picture to for it rather than for move select",
    },
    added = {
      "move names print whole: the tile font is 8px a glyph and a classic cell is seven of them against Gen 1's twelve-glyph names, so a move menu that will not fit is drawn in Plain Pixel -- the TTF the engine already ships for its translation mode -- at the largest size whose twelve glyphs still fit the cell",
      "a grid takes the small face for all four names or none, because GUST in one font beside THUNDERSHOCK in another reads as a rendering fault rather than a choice; a party whose names all fit is vanilla to the pixel, the command menu never moves, and the wide layout never reaches for it",
      "FULL NAMES in the mod manager, OFF by default -- the panel above already reads the whole name in the game's own font, so the buttons stay the game's own font cut to the cell, which is what the 2x2 has always looked like; on prints them whole in the buttons too",
      "opening the bag leaves the buttons up and marks ITEM with a hollow hand rather than clearing them away: the item list is the one list in the game that is not a screen of its own (ListMenu's itemBox, isOpaque = false, sixteen tiles at (4,2)), so it stops at y=103 and the strip underneath it is still on screen",
      "that state is deliberately not claimed through battle.bottom_ui_visible -- a false for the battle would take the bag's own How many?, YES/NO and use message with it, since every box above a battle inherits the battle's answer -- so the engine keeps drawing its empty box and the buttons go over the top of it, into the same pixels",
      "a panel above the move grid with the highlighted move's name, its type and its PP -- which the vanilla list never showed at once -- standing where the vanilla TYPE/PP box stood at (0,8), fourteen tiles by five with a row each for the three: twelve interior glyphs is exactly Gen 1's longest move name, so it never cuts, and it stops 48 pixels short of the full width, clear of the player's own HP numbers",
      "the move's type is drawn in that type's own colour in the panel, and each button's move name in its own, so the grid reads as four types at a glance and the panel says which one the cursor is on",
      "the letters themselves are coloured rather than a field behind them: a tile glyph is black on transparent and setColor cannot reach one, so a shader throws each glyph's RGB away and keeps only its alpha -- the glyph becomes a stencil filled with the type's colour, the same tile sheet in a different ink",
      "TYPE COLOUR in the mod manager, on by default; off is plain black text everywhere",
      "MOVE PANEL in the mod manager, which gives the picture behind it back, and on the widescreen layout gives its ten tiles to the grid rather than leaving a hole in the frame",
      "a Gen 2 style XP bar under the player's Pokemon, filling towards the next level with Gen 2's fill-hold-burst-refill on a level up, and stopping once that Pokemon faints and the engine clears the HUD out from under it. XP BAR in the mod manager, on by default",
      "the XP bar is drawn BEFORE the button grid in the same function, which is why it no longer lies across the move panel: it was Gen1WildQOL's until 1.3.0, drawn by a wrapper around battle.draw that runs after every battle.overlay link whatever priority they carry, so it could not be drawn over and clipped itself to x=88 -- the VANILLA panel's edge -- while this mod's panel ends at 112. An order needs no clip and stays right when the panel changes width",
      "the mod's geometry on mod.exports, in tile coordinates, so a mod that wants to sit beside these buttons can find out where they are, and panelRect(battle) in game pixels for anything that draws after this mod and must not be drawn over",
    },
    known = {
      "the XP bar's 3D-battle path is deliberately not carried over from Gen1WildQOL: it drew into another mod's canvas through a handshake with that mod's snapHUDs, and the handshake decided whether the path was taken at all, so porting it without the handshake would take that path whenever the other mod was loaded",
      "the small face is NOT the game's font, so a font mod or a skin that redraws the tile sheet does not reach it -- unlike the boxes, which are Font.drawBox and do; that is the trade FULL NAMES makes, and off is the way out of it",
      "the engine's own whole-game TTF mode is deliberately not switched on: that is a font swap for every screen in the game, and a battle mod has no business making one",
      "seven glyphs is seven glyphs: no arrangement of two columns inside 160 pixels gives THUNDERSHOCK its full name in a cell, which is what the panel above is for and what FULL NAMES is the other way out of",
      "the colour needs a shader, which is the one thing here a host can simply not have: without love.graphics.newShader the letters are black and nothing else changes, which is the picture this mod drew before it had any colour, and a type this mod has no colour for -- a mod's own -- is left black as well",
      "THROW ROCK is ten glyphs against seven in a Safari battle and reads THROW.; the widescreen layout has the room and prints it whole",
      "the overlay draws after the palette pass, so the buttons are black on white and are not recoloured by a COLORS zone the way the vanilla strip's border is -- the same picture on the default look, and not under a mode that tints the text box",
      "nothing about what the menus DO is replaced: every index, key and callback is still the engine's, so anything wrong with what the battle menu does is not this mod",
      "the strip is claimed only while the battle is the TOP of the state stack, because opening the party or the bag leaves the battle on phase menu the whole time that screen is up -- claiming on the phase alone would take those screens' own prompts down with it",
      "PKMN reaches the same parked path as ITEM and is never seen down it: PartyMenu is opaque, so the stack stops drawing at it and the battle underneath -- this mod's overlay included -- never runs at all",
      "a throw while drawing costs a frame's buttons and is logged; a LOAD-time failure is not swallowed, and marks the row enabled-but-broken in MODS with the reason, because an enabled mod that silently changes nothing looks exactly like one that was never installed",
    },
  },
}
